{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YWmJVJWXGp5h5aonWkvTVP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{array}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue,urlcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n14\t\n15\t\\definecolor{greenCell}{HTML}{C6EFCE}\n16\t\\definecolor{yellowCell}{HTML}{FFEB9C}\n17\t\\definecolor{redCell}{HTML}{FFC7CE}\n18\t\n19\t\\newcommand{\\contcal}{\\textsc{ContamCal}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n21\t\\newcommand{\\given}{\\mid}\n22\t\n23\t\\title{\\contcal{}: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination\\\\via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory}\n24\t\n25\t\\author{Anonymous}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t%% ============================================================\n31\t%% ABSTRACT\n32\t%% ============================================================\n33\t\\begin{abstract}\n34\tBenchmark scores for large language models are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination, yet existing detection methods flag contamination without producing corrected scores.\n35\tWe introduce \\contcal{}, a two-stage framework that (1)~maps per-item contamination exposure via Reciprocal Rank Fusion of four detection signals, and (2)~feeds these exposure scores into a contamination-augmented four-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model with per-benchmark contamination susceptibility parameters~$\\gamma_k$.\n36\tOn controlled contamination injection experiments across Qwen2.5 (0.5B--7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, \\contcal{} recovers uncontaminated benchmark performance with 2.1\\pp{} mean absolute error and 92\\% credible interval coverage, outperforming all seven baselines including DCR (4.6\\pp{}) and linear regression (4.3\\pp{}).\n37\tWe provide the first systematic signal$\\times$scale$\\times$dosage characterization, revealing that Min-K++ and Self-Critique signals are ineffective below ${\\sim}1.2$B parameters (AUC$\\approx$0.47) yet recover effectiveness at ${\\geq}1.5$B (AUC$\\geq$0.70).\n38\tCritically, Min-K++ exhibits \\emph{directional reversal} at high contamination (Cohen's $d=-2.67$ at 3B), actively misleading rather than merely failing.\n39\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52, cross-family $r=0.91$) reveals that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property, not a model property.\n40\tFisher Information analysis establishes structural (AUC$\\geq$0.55) and practical (AUC$\\geq$0.70) identifiability thresholds, validated by 144-condition simulation and cross-family experiments.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%% ============================================================\n44\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n45\t%% ============================================================\n46\t\\section{Introduction}\n47\t\n48\tTraining data contamination---the presence of benchmark test items in language model training corpora---undermines the validity of benchmark-based evaluation.\n49\tRephrased test items can inflate MMLU accuracy by 8--15 percentage points while evading standard $n$-gram decontamination \\citep{balloccu2024leak,dekoninck2024evading}.\n50\t\\citet{oren2024proving} demonstrated that all 20 mitigation strategies they tested fail to reliably eliminate contamination effects (the ``Emperor's New Benchmarks'' finding).\n51\tDynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \\citep{white2024livebench} offer one alternative, but require continuous curation at significant cost.\n52\t\n53\tExisting contamination detection methods---Min-K\\%++ \\citep{shi2024detecting}, ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat}, Self-Critique \\citep{weller2024selfcritique}---can identify contaminated items but do not produce calibrated scores.\n54\tStandard IRT approaches model item difficulty and model ability jointly \\citep{lalor2024benchmarking,polo2024tinybenchmarks} but do not account for contamination.\n55\tDCR \\citep{yang2025dcr} provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks uncertainty quantification.\n56\tNo existing method answers the counterfactual question: \\emph{what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?}\n57\t\n58\tWe propose \\contcal{}, a two-stage framework grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition \\citep{eckerly2017detecting,sinharay2017assessment} and explanatory IRT \\citep{deboeck2004explanatory}.\n59\tStage~1 computes per-item contamination exposure scores via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \\citep{cormack2009reciprocal} of four detection signals: embedding semantic retrieval (S1), Min-K\\%++ (S2), ConStat performance gap (S3), and Self-Critique entropy collapse (S4).\n60\tStage~2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters $\\gamma_k$, producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals.\n61\tCounterfactual scores are obtained by setting exposure to zero.\n62\t\n63\tOur contributions are threefold.\n64\t\\textbf{(1)}~The \\contcal{} framework recovers uncontaminated performance within 2.1\\pp{} MAE with 92\\% credible interval coverage, significantly outperforming seven baselines.\n65\t\\textbf{(2)}~A systematic signal$\\times$scale$\\times$dosage characterization reveals scale-dependent signal effectiveness and the Min-K++ directional reversal phenomenon (Cohen's $d$ up to $-2.85$), the paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding.\n66\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52) demonstrates that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property.\n67\t\\textbf{(3)}~Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishes structural and practical signal quality thresholds, validated across model families ($r=0.91$).\n68\t\n69\t\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}\n76\tMembership inference approaches detect whether specific items appeared in training data.\n77\tMin-K\\%++ \\citep{shi2024detecting} identifies memorized items via low-perplexity token subsets, building on MIA foundations \\citep{shokri2017membership,carlini2022quantifying}.\n78\tConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on original versus paraphrased items.\n79\tSelf-Critique prompts models to assess their own familiarity \\citep{weller2024selfcritique}.\n80\tKDS \\citep{dong2024generalization} uses knowledge-dependent signals.\n81\tThese methods flag contamination but do not produce calibrated scores.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Contamination Mitigation.}\n84\t\\citet{oren2024proving} systematically evaluated 20 mitigation strategies and found none effective, motivating post-hoc calibration.\n85\tDynamic benchmarks \\citep{white2024livebench} and human evaluation platforms \\citep{zheng2023judging} bypass contamination but at high cost.\n86\tHELM \\citep{liang2023holistic} provides standardized evaluation but does not address contamination.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{IRT for LLM Evaluation.}\n89\tItem Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation by several groups \\citep{lalor2024benchmarking,polo2024tinybenchmarks}.\n90\tHiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination.\n91\t\\citet{burnell2023rethink} explore multidimensional IRT for LLMs.\n92\tOur work extends this line by incorporating contamination covariates, connecting to the item compromise IRT literature in educational testing \\citep{eckerly2017detecting,sinharay2017assessment} and Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis \\citep{holland1993differential,thissen1993detection,zumbo1999handbook}.\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Post-hoc Calibration.}\n95\tDCR \\citep{yang2025dcr} provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks probabilistic uncertainty quantification.\n96\tOur $\\gamma_k$ parameter is analogous to DIF between exposed and unexposed groups.\n97\tWe note that ``calibration'' here refers to decontamination---correcting for systematic bias---not probability calibration in the sense of \\citet{guo2017calibration} or \\citet{platt1999probabilistic}.\n98\tConformal prediction \\citep{angelopoulos2023conformal} offers distribution-free coverage guarantees but assumes exchangeability violated by contamination's structured bias; Bayesian IRT naturally models this structure.\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Data Attribution.}\n101\tTracIn \\citep{pruthi2020estimating} and TRAK \\citep{park2023trak} attribute model predictions to training examples.\n102\tThese could serve as additional contamination signals in future work.\n103\t\n104\t\n105\t%% ============================================================\n106\t%% 3. METHOD\n107\t%% ============================================================\n108\t\\section{Method}\n109\t\n110\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n111\t\n112\tLet $\\theta_j$ denote the true (uncontaminated) ability of model $j$ on a benchmark, $e_{ij}\\in[0,1]$ the contamination exposure score for item $i$ and model $j$, and $\\gamma_k > 0$ the contamination susceptibility of benchmark $k$.\n113\tWe seek a calibrated score $\\hat{\\theta}_j$ such that the counterfactual accuracy (at $e_{ij}=0$ for all $i$) approximates $\\theta_j$.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Per-Item Exposure Mapping}\n116\t\n117\tWe compute four detection signals for each item--model pair:\n118\t\\begin{itemize}\n119\t\\item \\textbf{S1 (Embedding Retrieval):} Cosine similarity between the test item and the nearest training-set item in sentence-transformer embedding space. Scale-invariant (AUC$\\geq$0.95 across all model sizes).\n120\t\\item \\textbf{S2 (Min-K\\%++):} Token-level perplexity anomaly detection \\citep{shi2024detecting}. Scale-dependent: AUC$\\approx$0.47 at 0.5B, $\\geq$0.72 at $\\geq$1.5B.\n121\t\\item \\textbf{S3 (ConStat):} Performance gap between original and paraphrased items \\citep{dekoninck2024constat}. Available without logit access.\n122\t\\item \\textbf{S4 (Self-Critique):} Entropy collapse on familiar items \\citep{weller2024selfcritique}. Scale-dependent with threshold at ${\\sim}1.4$B.\n123\t\\end{itemize}\n124\t\n125\tSignals are aggregated via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF; \\citealt{cormack2009reciprocal}):\n126\t\\begin{equation}\n127\t\\text{RRF}(i) = \\sum_{s=1}^{S} \\frac{1}{k + r_s(i)}\n128\t\\end{equation}\n129\twhere $r_s(i)$ is the rank of item $i$ under signal $s$ and $k=60$ is the standard smoothing constant.\n130\tRRF is training-free, avoiding meta-circularity: a learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated.\n131\tWhen constituent signals fail (e.g., S2/S4 at 0.5B), RRF gracefully degrades to the dominant signal.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Stage 2: Contamination-Augmented 4PL IRT}\n134\t\n135\tWe augment the standard four-parameter logistic IRT model \\citep{deboeck2004explanatory} with a contamination term.\n136\tThe probability of model $j$ answering item $i$ correctly on benchmark $k$ is:\n137\t\\begin{equation}\n138\tP(Y_{ij}=1) = c_i + \\frac{d_i - c_i}{1 + \\exp\\!\\bigl(-a_i(\\theta_j - b_i + \\gamma_k \\cdot e_{ij})\\bigr)}\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\twhere $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ item difficulty, $c_i$ the guessing parameter, $d_i$ the upper asymptote, $\\theta_j$ model ability, $\\gamma_k$ per-benchmark contamination susceptibility, and $e_{ij}$ the exposure score from Stage~1.\n141\t\n142\tThe counterfactual (calibrated) score for model $j$ is:\n143\t\\begin{equation}\n144\t\\hat{\\theta}_j^{\\text{cal}} = \\frac{1}{N_k}\\sum_{i=1}^{N_k} P(Y_{ij}=1 \\given e_{ij}=0, \\hat{\\theta}_j, \\hat{a}_i, \\hat{b}_i, \\hat{c}_i, \\hat{d}_i)\n145\t\\end{equation}\n146\t\n147\t\\paragraph{Measurement Error Model.}\n148\tExposure scores $e_{ij}$ are noisy estimates of latent true exposure $\\tau_{ij}$.\n149\tWe model $e_{ij} = \\tau_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ij}$ where $\\epsilon_{ij} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2_e)$ and propagate this uncertainty through the posterior via a measurement error (ME) model \\citep{carroll2006measurement}.\n150\tModels with high-AUC signals receive tighter credible intervals; those with degraded signals receive appropriately wider intervals.\n151\t\n152\t\\paragraph{Priors and Inference.}\n153\tWe fit per-benchmark models using Stan NUTS \\citep{carpenter2017stan} with weakly informative priors: $a_i \\sim \\text{LogNormal}(0, 0.5)$, $b_i \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $c_i \\sim \\text{Beta}(2, 8)$, $d_i \\sim \\text{Beta}(8, 2)$, $\\theta_j \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $\\gamma_k \\sim \\text{HalfNormal}(0, 1)$.\n154\tModels are pooled across scales and seeds ($N_{\\text{models}} = 32$).\n155\tConvergence is verified by $\\hat{R} < 1.01$ and ESS $> 380$ for all parameters.\n156\t\n157\t\\paragraph{Identifiability Analysis.}\n158\tFisher Information for $\\gamma_k$ depends on signal quality (via $e_{ij}$ variance) and sample size.\n159\tWe define a \\emph{structural threshold} (AUC$\\geq$0.55: $\\gamma$ is technically identifiable) and a \\emph{practical threshold} (AUC$\\geq$0.70: credible intervals are usefully narrow), validated by 144-condition simulation.\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{Local Independence.}\n162\tStandard IRT assumes independent examinees; a single LLM produces all responses, inducing correlation through shared parameters \\citep{mislevy1986bayes}.\n163\tWe mitigate by (1)~fitting per-benchmark (reducing correlation scope), (2)~pooling models across scales and seeds, and (3)~reporting SBC diagnostics \\citep{talts2020validating} to verify model specification.\n164\t\n165\t\n166\t%% ============================================================\n167\t%% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n168\t%% ============================================================\n169\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n170\t\n171\t\\paragraph{Controlled Contamination Injection.}\n172\tWe fine-tune Qwen2.5 models at four scales (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B) using LoRA SFT at four contamination dosage levels: d000 (clean), d005 (5\\% items contaminated), d025 (25\\%), d100 (100\\%).\n173\tEach condition is run with 2--3 random seeds.\n174\tMulti-seed pooling yields $N_{\\text{models}} \\geq 32$.\n175\t\n176\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n177\tMMLU (1,000 items subsampled), ARC-Challenge (1,172 items), GSM8K (1,319 items; negative control---open-ended format resists contamination), and HumanEval (164 items; appendix only).\n178\t\n179\t\\paragraph{Baselines (7).}\n180\t(0)~Raw scores, (1)~Remove-and-rescore, (2)~Single-signal 2PL+$\\gamma$, (3)~Vanilla 4PL ($\\gamma{=}0$), (4)~Linear exposure regression, (5)~Emperor mitigation strategies \\citep{oren2024proving}, (6)~DCR \\citep{yang2025dcr}.\n181\t\n182\t\\paragraph{Evaluation Metrics (6).}\n183\tMAE (pp), 95\\%/80\\%/50\\% credible interval coverage, CI sharpness (width), Kendall's $\\tau$, dose-response monotonicity, and SBC diagnostic \\citep{talts2020validating}.\n184\t\n185\t\n186\t%% ============================================================\n187\t%% 4.1 SIGNAL PIPELINE\n188\t%% ============================================================\n189\t\\subsection{Detection Signal Effectiveness}\n190\t\\label{sec:signals}\n191\t\n192\tWe compute all four detection signals across 0.5B/1.5B/3B models and evaluate item-level discrimination via within-model AUC at d005.\n193\tTable~\\ref{tab:signal_auc} presents the full signal$\\times$scale$\\times$benchmark matrix.\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[t]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\small\n198\t\\caption{Within-model AUC at d005 for each signal$\\times$scale$\\times$benchmark. \\colorbox{greenCell}{Green}: AUC$\\geq$0.70; \\colorbox{yellowCell}{Yellow}: 0.55--0.70; \\colorbox{redCell}{Red}: $<$0.55.}\n199\t\\label{tab:signal_auc}\n200\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}"}]}